    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 101114\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n414 AM PDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* An incoming system will bring widespread precipitation chances and \n  an elevated flash flood risk to the area through the weekend. \n\n* This system will also bring gusty winds to the region over the \n  weekend into early next week.\n\n* Cooler than normal temperatures will stick around through next \n  week. &&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Friday.\n\nWe continue to monitor Rachel in the East Pacific and the incoming \ntrough, currently over British Columbia. As this trough begins to \ndig south today and tomorrow, Rachel will continue tracking \nnortheast towards the coast of Southern California before lifting \ninto southern California and up the Colorado River Valley overnight. \nAs Rachel's circulation approaches the area today, we precipitation \nchances begin to increase in the afternoon and evening. Showers and \nthunderstorms will become more widespread overnight Saturday into \nSunday as the circulation moves overhead. Heavy rain, flash \nflooding, and strong winds will be the primary threats with these \nstorms associated with Rachel's circulation. Additionally, the Storm \nPrediction Center (SPC) has highlighted southern Mohave County, the \nColorado River Valley, and southern Nevada with a 2% tornado risk. \nIf a tornado were to occur, it would be a short-lived spin up on the \nlower end of the EF-scale. The aforementioned trough will push also \npush into the area on Sunday as Rachel moves through. Cold air \naloft, combined with remnant moisture, and dynamic forcing aloft \nwill help fuel widespread storms across the region on Sunday. Given \nthe widespread threat of flash flooding, the Flash Flood Watches for \nmost of the area will remain in effect until early Monday morning. \nPrecipitation will linger through Monday across our eastern zones, \ntapering off on Tuesday. \n\nIn addition to heavy rain and flash flooding, strong southerly winds \nare also expected across the region beginning later today across the \nsouthern Great Basin as the trough begins to dig south. Winds across \nthe southern half of our forecast area will pick up overnight/early \nSunday morning as Rachel's circulation moves through the area. The \nlatest forecast has 40+ mph wind gusts starting earlier than the \nprevious forecast package. Given this earlier onset of strong winds, \nwe have pushed the start time of the Wind Advisory for San \nBernardino, Mohave, and Clark Counties from 5:00 am Sunday to 2:00 \nam Sunday. Overall, wind gusts are expected to peak in the 45 to 55 \nmph range, outside of any convective influence (thunderstorm winds). \nHowever, guidance continues to show a dry slot forming over Mohave \nCounty early Sunday morning as Rachel's circulation moves up the \nColorado River Valley. This will open the door for stronger winds \naloft to mix down to the surface. Winds will be elevated today and \nMonday, peaking on Sunday.\n\nAside from heavy rain, flash flooding, and strong winds, cooler than \nnormal temperatures will also accompany this weekend's unsettled \nweather. Temperatures will drop around 10 to 15 degrees from today \nto tomorrow, and will continue to cool through at least Tuesday. \nBeyond Tuesday, temperatures look to slowly recover during the \nsecond half of the week. However, despite this gradual warm up, \ntemperatures will remain cooler than normal as we head into the \nweekend.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Light \nsouthwest winds will continue through mid-morning before turning \nmore south to southwest and becoming gusty. A few gusts of 25 to 30 \nknots are possible through the afternoon, especially near any shower \nactivity that begins to move into the valley after 21Z. Shower \nactivity will increase through the evening and overnight, with the \npotential for convection increasing after 06Z. Confidence in the \nwind forecast is low from this evening onward as convective \ninfluences and the remnant circulation of Rachel approach the \nregion. Generally, winds should favor southerly components, but \nquite a bit of variability is likely. Shower and thunderstorm \nactivity will persist into Sunday. VFR conditions will persist into \nthe afternoon, but CIGs falling below 10kft AGL are likely by mid-\nevening and could fall as low as 6kft to 8kft at times overnight and \nSunday morning in areas of heavier precipitation. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Deteriorating aviation \nconditions are expected across the region today as moisture from the \nremnants of Rachel starts to spread across the area. Initially this \nmorning, light winds are expected at most regional TAF sites. \nHowever, most areas will see increasing south to southwest winds \nthis afternoon, with showers and isolated areas of convection \nspreading south to north across much of the area this afternoon and \nevening. VFR conditions will persist into the afternoon with cloud \nbases remaining above 12kft AGL. CIGs are likely to fall below 10kft \nAGL by mid-evening and could fall as low as 6kft to 8kft at times \novernight and Sunday morning in areas of heavier precipitation.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Stessman\nAVIATION...Planz\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
